// logos.std.wql.join_order — WHICH SIDE DRIVES THE JOIN (ADR 0024 S4).
//
// A left-deep join has a BASE — the outermost loop, read once, scanned whole —
// and STEPS, each indexed or rescanned per bound row. Until this module existed
// the base was whatever the query wrote first, and that is the one join decision
// where a STATIC answer cannot be right: which side is cheaper to index depends
// on how many rows each side holds, and no compile-time fact says. That is the
// difference between this axis and the ACCESS axis, where a runtime decision is
// nearly always the same answer (a narrowing over an ordered source is never
// meaningfully worse than a scan — one descent against zero, never more rows).
//
// So the answer is not a better compile-time guess. It is TWO orders in the
// emitted code and a DISCRIMINANT the plan owns: the sizes the plan asked for
// (S4g) are compared once and the branch taken indexes the SMALLER side and
// walks the larger.
//
// ⚠ THAT COMPARISON IS NOT MADE HERE AND NOT IN THE QUERY'S BODY. It is made in
// the query's PREPARE fn and recorded on the plan object (ADR 0024 S4i,
// `logos.std.wql.prepared`) — decided once, applied many times, which is what
// makes it a plan rather than a per-call test. What this module hands over is
// therefore the two size EXPRESSIONS plus the ground.
//
// ⚠ AND THERE ARE TWO MOMENTS TO HAND THEM OVER FOR (ADR 0024 S4j). S4i read its
// own tightening too widely: a size that only exists after the query's prelude
// has run is unusable IN `prepare`, and that is all it means. Inside `run`, past
// the prelude and before the join's index, a materialized rel's `.len()` is the
// same field read the nest's own loop condition makes — free. So this module
// answers TWICE, in order: first with what `prepare` can have (`size_expr_of`),
// and where that fails, with what `run` can have for nothing
// (`run_size_expr_of`). A refusal survives only where NEITHER point has a number,
// which after this slice means exactly one thing: a side that STREAMS, whose
// length would cost draining the iterator — the query's own work traded for a
// plan fact, the error this axis exists to avoid.
//
// ⚠ WHAT REORDERING IS NOT ALLOWED TO DO IS CHANGE THE ANSWER, and for a join
// the answer includes the ORDER of the rows. Deem's default row order IS the
// nest's: `from a x join b y` yields a-major, and every e2e expectation in the
// tree reads that way. Driving from `b` yields the same multiset in b-major
// order — the same rows, a different sequence, which is a semantic change and
// exactly the failure mode that does not crash. So the reorder is offered only
// where the query names an order of its own AND the emitted sort is TOTAL:
// `order by` collects the matched row-index tuples and sorts them, and with the
// tuple itself as the tiebreak (in QUERY source order — `sort_perm_frag`) the
// sorted sequence is the same whichever nest produced it. Without `order by`
// there is nothing to restore the sequence to, and the plan says so instead of
// reordering anyway.
//
// The other refusals are semantic, not conservative: an ANTI join is not
// symmetric (rows of `a` with no matching `b` is a different question from rows
// of `b` with no matching `a`), and a TRAVERSAL step's source is a field path of
// an outer row, so it cannot be an outermost loop at all — there is nothing to
// bind its base to.
package logos.std.wql.join_order;

use logos.lang.str;
use logos.mem.string;
use logos.lang.writ.container;    // Writ
use logos.lang.writ.anyval;       // WAny
use logos.std.wql.ir;             // SExpr / WRef
use logos.std.wql.el;             // ElTypes
use logos.std.wql.codegen;        // plan_trace / string_as_str — the ONE decision channel
use logos.std.wql.params;         // MacroParams — the rel registry (sizes, streaming)
use logos.std.wql.join_sel;       // decide_join_step / js_reads_once / js_name / StepSel

// ── The decision ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `dynamic` — the emitted code carries BOTH orders and picks at run time;
// otherwise the query's own order is the only one emitted (no code growth for a
// query that cannot use a second one). `why` is the ground either way, because
// "the plan left this order alone" is the answer a person reading a slow join
// needs and it is the one a decision recorded only on success cannot give.
//
// `alt_*` is the transposed order's decision for the step it moves, made HERE
// and stamped onto the reordered chain rather than re-derived by the emitter:
// the moved step binds a different row var, so its equi-key and therefore its
// strategy are a different question from the one the planner already answered,
// and both answers come out of the one copy of the rule (`join_sel`).
//
// `deferred` — the two nests exist, but the DISCRIMINANT is not a field of the
// prepared plan: the numbers only become free inside `run` (ADR 0024 S4j), so the
// comparison is made there, once, above the nest. `dynamic` still means "two
// nests are emitted" either way; `deferred` says WHICH of the two decision points
// filled the discriminant, and the emitter reads exactly that.
pub struct JoinOrder {
    pub dynamic:   bool,
    pub deferred:  bool,
    pub why:       str,
    pub alt_strat: i32,
    pub alt_key:   i32,
    pub alt_sel:   i64,
    pub alt_why:   str,
}

fn jo_fixed(why: str) -> JoinOrder {
    return JoinOrder { dynamic: false, deferred: false, why: why, alt_strat: JS_NONE(),
                       alt_key: 0i32, alt_sel: -1i64, alt_why: "" };
}

/// A source's SIZE, as an expression evaluable in the PREPARE fn — that is,
/// before the query's own prelude has run (ADR 0024 S4i).
///
/// Two spellings, and the order matters. A source that DECLARED a size operation
/// has already been asked (`AccessPlan.size_at`, S4g) — the number is one
/// reporter call on the source PARAMETER, which `prepare` can make for itself;
/// `at` receives the rel index so the caller can emit that one binding and
/// nothing else. Anything else is asked the way the nest's own loop condition
/// asks: `.len()` on an indexable source.
///
/// ⚠ AND THAT SECOND SPELLING IS ONLY AVAILABLE ON A PARAMETER. A rel's slice
/// (`__rel_x_sl`) is a local of the generated fn, produced by materializing the
/// relation — so its length is not a fact about the input data but a result of
/// the query's own work, and a plan prepared before that work has no number to
/// compare. Such a side reports no size here, exactly like a STREAMED source
/// (whose length costs draining the iterator, the one thing streaming exists to
/// avoid).
fn size_expr_of(prm: &MacroParams, src: str, out: &mut String, at: &mut i64) -> bool {
    *at = -1i64;
    if prm.rel_src_streams(src) { return false; }
    let ri: i64 = prm.rel_find_src(src);
    if ri >= 0i64 {
        if prm.rel_size_at[ri].len() == 0i64 { return false; }
        *at = ri;
        out.push_str("(");
        out.push_str(prm.rel_size_at[ri]);
        out.push_str(" as i64)");
        return true;
    }
    out.push_str("(");
    out.push_str(src);
    out.push_str(").len()");
    return true;
}

/// A source's SIZE as an expression evaluable INSIDE `run` — in the region between
/// the prelude's materializations and the join's index build (ADR 0024 S4j, the
/// SECOND decision point).
///
/// ⚠ ONE SPELLING, and that is the point: `(<src>).len()`, which is the SAME
/// expression the nest's own base loop condition reads (`chain_nest_frag`). So the
/// number is not obtained, it is already there — a length field of a binding the
/// prelude made because the query needed it. That is what "free" means here, and
/// it is the only ground on which deferring is allowed to buy anything: nothing
/// about the query's cost moves.
///
/// ⚠ A STREAMED SIDE STILL HAS NO NUMBER. An iterator has no length, and draining
/// it to count is the query's own work — exactly what streaming exists to avoid,
/// and exactly the trade this axis forbids. Such a side reports none at BOTH
/// points, so its join keeps one nest and the refusal keeps its ground.
///
/// ⚠ AND THAT GUARD CANNOT FIRE TODAY, which is a fact worth writing down rather
/// than a reason to drop it. Streaming requires the ABSENCE of `order by`
/// (`plan_walker::plan_mark_single_pass`: a sort re-binds joined rows by position,
/// so every side must be indexable) and a transposition requires its PRESENCE — the
/// two conditions exclude each other, so every side reaching this function is
/// materialized. The check stays because the coupling is another module's decision
/// and a streaming order-preserving sort would end it silently; a function that is
/// right only because of a distant invariant it does not name is the phase-proxy
/// anti-pattern. `wql_deferred_plan_e2e` measures the consequence instead: an
/// ITERATOR producer whose length is free because the sort drained it anyway, with
/// the pull count pinning that the deferral added no second drain.
///
/// ⚠ A DECLARED SIZE REPORTER IS NOT CONSULTED HERE. At this point the rel is
/// materialized, so its length is the exact count rather than a reported estimate,
/// and it costs less than the call would. `size_expr_of` is the one that asks the
/// declaration, because before the query runs the declaration is all there is.
fn run_size_expr_of(prm: &MacroParams, src: str, out: &mut String) -> bool {
    if prm.rel_src_streams(src) { return false; }
    out.push_str("(");
    out.push_str(src);
    out.push_str(").len()");
    return true;
}

/// Decide whether the FIRST join of a chain may be driven from either side.
///
/// The candidate transposition is always the first two sources, and that is not
/// a restriction of convenience: swapping positions 0 and 1 leaves every LATER
/// step's bound set exactly as it was (both vars are bound by position 1 either
/// way), so no later predicate moves and no later strategy changes. Any other
/// permutation can force two predicates onto one position, and a step carries
/// one `on`.
///
/// Returns the decision. `bsz`/`ssz` receive the two side SIZES as expressions
/// and `bat`/`sat` the rel whose declared reporter each one calls (-1 for a
/// parameter's own `.len()`), so the PREPARE fn can bind exactly the reporters
/// its comparison reads. The comparison itself is NOT built here: it is made in
/// one place per decision point — the emitted `prepare` for a prepared decision
/// (which is also where its result is recorded on the plan, so the branch reads
/// the plan's field and cannot disagree with the number the plan reports, ADR 0024
/// S4i), or the one deferred binding above the nest for a deferred one (S4j). When
/// `deferred` comes back true, `bat`/`sat` are -1 and the expressions in
/// `bsz`/`ssz` are to be evaluated in `run`, not in `prepare`.
pub fn decide_join_order(nsteps: i64, base_src: str, base_var: str,
                         s0_src: str, s0_var: str, s0_on: WRef<SExpr>,
                         s0_edge: bool, s0_anti: bool, s0_strat: i32,
                         has_sort: bool, t: &ElTypes, prm: &MacroParams,
                         h: &Writ, bsz: &mut String, ssz: &mut String,
                         bat: &mut i64, sat: &mut i64) -> JoinOrder {
    let mut jo: JoinOrder = jo_fixed("");
    if nsteps < 1i64 {
        return jo_fixed("no join step — there is no second side to drive from");
    }
    if s0_edge {
        jo = jo_fixed("a traversal step's source is a field path of an outer row, so it cannot be an outermost loop — there is nothing to bind its base to");
    } else if s0_anti {
        jo = jo_fixed("an anti join is not symmetric: the rows of the base with no match are a different question from the rows of the step with no match");
    } else if !has_sort {
        jo = jo_fixed("the row order of a join IS the nest's, and this query names no other one — without `order by` there is no sequence to restore, so driving the other side would return the same rows in a different order");
    } else if s0_strat == JS_NONE() {
        jo = jo_fixed("no plan decided this step's strategy (a rel body's own chain is emitted per fixpoint variant), so there is no decision to offer a second order for");
    } else if str_eq(base_src, s0_src) {
        // ⚠ A SELF-JOIN HAS NOTHING TO MEASURE, at either decision point. Both
        // sides are the same binding, so the two numbers are equal by
        // construction and the comparison can only ever answer "do not swap" —
        // a second nest that no data can select. Said here rather than reached
        // through the size machinery, because the size machinery would answer
        // "unmeasurable" and that is the wrong reason: the sizes are perfectly
        // knowable, they are simply the same one.
        jo = jo_fixed("both sides are the same source, so the two sizes are equal by construction — no measurement at either decision point can prefer one order, and the transposed nest could never be the one chosen");
    } else {
        // The REVERSE direction's own decision: the moved step binds the base's
        // row var, so its equi-key is selected against a different new-side.
        let alt: StepSel = decide_join_step(s0_on, base_var, t, h);
        if !js_reads_once(alt.strat) {
            jo = jo_fixed("the reverse direction has no equi-key that can be indexed, so driving the other side would rescan a source the query order reads once");
        } else {
            // ⚠ BOTH sides are asked, even though the first failure decides:
            // `size_expr_of` writes into the caller's buffers, so short-circuiting
            // would leave one half-filled and the ground would then depend on the
            // order of the operands.
            let okb: bool = size_expr_of(prm, base_src, bsz, bat);
            let oks: bool = size_expr_of(prm, s0_src, ssz, sat);
            if !okb || !oks {
                bsz.clear();
                ssz.clear();
                *bat = -1i64;
                *sat = -1i64;
                // ── POINT (2): the number `prepare` cannot have may be FREE in
                // `run` (ADR 0024 S4j). Asked only after point (1) has failed,
                // and in that order on purpose: a decision that can be REUSED is
                // worth more than one that is right per call, so the reusable one
                // is tried first and the deferral is the fallback, never the
                // preference.
                let rokb: bool = run_size_expr_of(prm, base_src, bsz);
                let roks: bool = run_size_expr_of(prm, s0_src, ssz);
                if !rokb || !roks {
                    bsz.clear();
                    ssz.clear();
                    jo = jo_fixed("a side's size is free at NEITHER decision point: it streams, so it has no length before the query runs and none inside it either — counting would mean draining the iterator, which is the query's own work, and trading that for a plan fact is what this axis refuses");
                } else {
                    jo = JoinOrder { dynamic: true, deferred: true,
                                     why: "either side may drive, but not on any fact `prepare` can hold: both sizes are LENGTHS OF RELATIONS THIS QUERY MATERIALIZES ANYWAY, so the order is decided inside `run` — once per call, above the nest, from numbers that cost nothing there. Per-call means not reusable: `agrees` and `margin` do not speak about this half",
                                     alt_strat: alt.strat, alt_key: alt.key_ty,
                                     alt_sel: alt.sel, alt_why: alt.why };
                }
            } else {
                jo = JoinOrder { dynamic: true, deferred: false,
                                 why: "either side may drive: the plan indexes the SMALLER side and walks the larger, decided once in `prepare` from the sizes the plan asked for — the sort's tuple tiebreak makes both nests yield the same sequence",
                                 alt_strat: alt.strat, alt_key: alt.key_ty,
                                 alt_sel: alt.sel, alt_why: alt.why };
            }
        }
    }
    // Recorded at the moment of deciding, like every other plan decision: a
    // returned "fixed" has already lost the antecedent that fixed it.
    //
    // ⚠ THE VERDICT NAMES THE DECISION POINT, not just the outcome. "drive either
    // side" and "drive either side in `run`" are two different claims about a plan
    // — the first is reusable, the second is remade every call — and a reader must
    // be able to tell them apart from the trace alone.
    let mut verdict: str = "drive fixed";
    if jo.dynamic {
        verdict = "drive either side";
        if jo.deferred { verdict = "drive either side in `run`"; }
    }
    plan_trace(base_var, verdict, s0_var, jo.why);
    if jo.deferred {
        // WHICH RELATION SUPPLIED THE NUMBER. The plan's own facts are -1 for a
        // deferred decision (nothing was measured when it was prepared), so the
        // two expressions are the only record of what will be compared and where
        // it comes from. Rebuilt through the same rule that produced them, so the
        // trace cannot name one thing while the code reads another.
        let mut sb: String = String::new();
        sb.push_str("compared in `run` after the prelude, before the join's index: ");
        let _tb: bool = run_size_expr_of(prm, base_src, &mut sb);
        sb.push_str(" vs ");
        let _ts: bool = run_size_expr_of(prm, s0_src, &mut sb);
        sb.push_str(" — each is the length of a binding the prelude already made, so the read is one field and the per-row cost does not move");
        plan_trace(base_var, "sizes deferred to run", s0_var, string_as_str(&sb));
    }
    if jo.dynamic {
        let mut ab: String = String::new();
        ab.push_str(js_name(jo.alt_strat));
        ab.push_str(" when driven from ");
        ab.push_str(s0_var);
        plan_trace(base_var, string_as_str(&ab), "", jo.alt_why);
    }
    return jo;
}
